    "id": "lem:effective-loading-mean-bound",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\) and every \\(c\\in[C]\\),\n\\[\n\\|m_c\\|_2^2+\\operatorname{tr}(H_c)\\le M_{\\mathrm{mom}}^{2/q_0},\n\\qquad\n\\|m_c\\|_2\\le b_\\lambda,\n\\qquad\n\\max_{t\\in[T]}|\\mu_{ct}(P)|\\le b_\\lambda.\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "Mclass",
      "C",
      "lambda",
      "mload",
      "H",
      "q0",
      "Mmom",
      "vstar",
      "blambda",
      "Gamma",
      "mu",
      "T"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:base-model",
      "def:effective-loading-mean-radius",
      "ass:moment-radius",
      "ass:loading-covariance",
      "ass:factor-normalization"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\) and \\(c\\in[C]\\).  The exponent \\(q_0=4+\\delta\\) in \\(\\operatorname{def:effective\\text{-}loading\\text{-}mean\\text{-}radius}\\) is larger than two because \\(\\delta>0\\).  By the definition of the conditional mean and covariance,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left(\\|\\lambda_i\\|_2^2\\mid C_i=c\\right)\n=\\|m_c\\|_2^2+\\operatorname{tr}(H_c).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nLyapunov's inequality, followed by the nonnegativity of the error moment and \\(\\operatorname{ass:moment\\text{-}radius}\\), gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\{\\|m_c\\|_2^2+\\operatorname{tr}(H_c)\\right\\}^{q_0/2}\n&=\\left\\{\\mathbb E_P(\\|\\lambda_i\\|_2^2\\mid C_i=c)\\right\\}^{q_0/2}\\\\\n&\\le \\mathbb E_P(\\|\\lambda_i\\|_2^{q_0}\\mid C_i=c)\\\\\n&\\le \\mathbb E_P(\\|\\lambda_i\\|_2^{q_0}+\\|\\varepsilon_i\\|_2^{q_0}\\mid C_i=c)\\\\\n&\\le M_{\\mathrm{mom}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nTaking the power \\(2/q_0>0\\) in (2) proves the first displayed inequality in the statement.  By \\(\\operatorname{ass:loading\\text{-}covariance}\\), both eigenvalues of \\(H_c\\) are at least \\(v_\\star\\), and therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{tr}(H_c)\\ge 2v_\\star.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nCombining (2)--(3) yields\n\\[\n\\|m_c\\|_2^2\n\\le M_{\\mathrm{mom}}^{2/q_0}-2v_\\star.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe left side of (4) is nonnegative.  Hence the right side is nonnegative for every nonempty model, and the definition of \\(b_\\lambda\\) gives \\(\\|m_c\\|_2\\le b_\\lambda\\).  Finally, \\(\\operatorname{ass:factor\\text{-}normalization}\\) implies \\(\\|\\Gamma\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=1\\).  Since \\(\\mu_c(P)=\\Gamma m_c\\), for each \\(t\\in[T]\\),\n\\[\n|\\mu_{ct}(P)|\n=|e_t^{\\prime}\\Gamma m_c|\n\\le \\|e_t^{\\prime}\\Gamma\\|_2\\|m_c\\|_2\n\\le \\|\\Gamma\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\|m_c\\|_2\n\\le b_\\lambda.\n\\]\nThis proves all three assertions.",
